  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gusty conditions continue today with rain chances returning to \nthe coast as a nor'easter strengthens this weekend.\n\nThe nor'easter continues to strengthen offshore, tracking towards NJ \nlater this weekend before wobbling up the New England coast early \nnext week. Confidence is high in continued impacts locally, \nespecially for the Eastern Shore. The position of the low, as well \nas the strong high pressure over the Great lakes, allows gusty winds \nto continue again today across the coast. Wind gusts of 35-40 mph \nare possible along the immediate coast (coastal MD Eastern Shore \ncould see gusts to 45 mph briefly this morning). Wind Advisories are \nin effect through 11 AM this morning along the Eastern Shore coastal \nwaters (Worcester, Accomack, and Northampton counties). At this \ntime, these will be allowed to expire, as winds begin to decrease \nslightly during the day. Interior portions will also see gusts to 25-\n30 mph this afternoon from well atmospheric mixing. Winds will \ngradually diminish tonight through Sunday, but remaining breezy with \ngusts to 30 mph along the coast. High pressure will then build into \nthe area Monday, helping kick the nor'easter further offshore \nbringing lighter winds back. \n\nRain chances will build back into the area from the NE as the low \nslides north and west. The steadier rain will likely remain on the \nEastern Shore where QPF is between 0.5-1.0\". For other coastal \nareas, the rain should stay on the lighter side closer to drizzle \nwith QPF under 0.25\". This late afternoon/evening, rain/drizzle \ncould push as far west as the piedmont based on the latest hi-res \nmodels. Rain chances will linger mostly for the Eastern Shore on \nSunday, but otherwise drying out. \n\nHigh pressure takes over next week leading to calmer weather. \nLooking at dry conditions and temperatures warming back into the 80s \nthrough the week. Above average temperatures in the mid to upper 80s \nare possible for some late week as ridging builds over the \nsoutheast.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread moderate to major tidal flooding \n(inundation of 2-3+ feet AGL in vulnerable areas) will continue into \nSaturday night or early Sunday morning at most spots, with some \nlocations especially in the middle Bay seeing flooding persist \nthrough the weekend.\n\nAs a Nor'easter continues to develop off the coast, winds have \ncontinued to back over the region, now closer to N to NNE. In \naddition, the speeds have also decreased slightly at 20-30 kt with \ngusts to 35 kt. However, this has done little to change the coastal \nflood situation with most sites still seeing anomalies of 2.5-3.5 ft \nabove normal astronomical tide. With little change overnight through \nSaturday, expect similar anomalies to persist. These anomalies \ncombined with higher astronomical tides will likely will allow for \ncontinued rounds of Moderate to Major tidal flooding across a \nmajority of the tidal sites through Saturday.\n\nOverall, storm surge guidance has trended even higher with the 12z \nruns, especially inside the Chesapeake Bay, for overnight and \nSaturday. This is due to the models trending west and slightly \nstronger with the Nor'easter. This will lead to a more N-NW flow \ninside the bay leading to more ebbing at the mouth of the Bay, it is \nalso possible that especially the eastern and southern shores of the \nChesapeake Bay could see increased water levels with a better \nonshore component to the winds. The tide forecast has been raised \nslightly for most sides, with the most substantial changes (~0.5 ft) \nacross aforementioned areas. Coastal Flood Warnings were extended out \nanother cycle, Saturday night-Sunday morning, and additional \nextensions are likely, especially across the middle Bay.",
